**Theorem 3.4 (explicit legacy protocol embedding).** On canonical
installations the map from an old typed input script to the new script
that keeps every outer choice and source frame, and inserts a separately
supplied HistoryMode zero before each whole old history frame, preserves
the old parser's frame classification and its strict-Zero selector meaning.
Complete old rejected frames stay rejected in that branch. Returning source
calls keep their original source effects and historical guards. This is
an embedding of protocols and corresponding source occurrences/phases;
it asserts no equality of raw old/new execution words, times, addresses,
code captures, history outputs or complete response maps.

Proof. The outer choice receiver is unchanged, including the first-zero
second bit. Its choice1 still grants only the original history invocation
slot. Definition 3.1 consumes exactly the new typed bit and retains it;
the next IN is the first original frame-header IN. Mode zero dispatches
to the original grammar, tests and failure order. Its supplied $m,z,s$
therefore refer to the corresponding acquired strict source occurrence
and the same local Zero phase. Native row numbering, global tallies and
origins in that occurrence are those of the new run, so its Issue binds
the new run's actual $n_0+5z+s$, not an old absolute ordinal. Source01
executes the same separately inlined $F^A$ and CAP branches, with the same
source packet/guard relation. History work touches no CAP event counter
or source writer. Induction over returning cycles proves these assertions.

The distinction is necessary for the claimed rejection preservation.
For example an old complete valid-$m$ selector with $s=5$ executes its
old invalid-phase/range rejection. Reinterpreting that identical frame as
success in the same branch would contradict preservation of that outcome.
Here its mode-zero behavior is retained; mode-one frames are distinguishable
by actually supplied data before their header. The outer prefix code
$\{00,01,1\}$ is unchanged and gains no fourth word. Extra reception,
registers, code and bank work alter actual clocks and bindings; equality
of entire wire outputs is neither a premise nor a consequence. ∎

